ferent  variant detection assays  and is abbott developing a variant or a  sequencing assay  so i think you know sequencing is  critical  in looking for new variants and see  because uh when you  you're if you've got a test that's  specific for certain mutations that have  been very strained defining variants or  mutations then  uh you will miss anything else that's  out there so sequencing remains kind of  the backbone for looking at  emerging new variants i think you can  develop assays  to pick up specific mutations  that's only really clinically relevant  if you're going to do something with  that information in terms of treat  people differently quarantined  you know behave differently so right now  those are kind of epidemiological  tools um i think they're they're they're  there's a few of them that are coming  out  uh and and so we're looking at that in  terms of  whether or not we would build one it it  really depends on the clinical utility  you know in terms of moving it forward  in a regulatory process as opposed to a  research tool  thank you dr clardy do you have any  final comments for our audience  no i'm very grateful for people to who  took the time to to spend this time with  us and and uh participate  in this call and for the interesting uh  questions that that they posed and uh  we're very  open to collaboration if anybody wants  to reach out as  i mentioned through scientific affairs  local reps or directly to us  we look forward to hearing from you  thank you again dr clardy for your time  today and for your important research  we would also like to thank lab roots  and our sponsor  global scientific affairs molecular  diagnostics at abbott  for underwriting today's educational  webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period  will be addressed by the speaker via the  contact information yo